WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.669
A code review should do more than summarize a pull request.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:08.339
It should show what changed, why it matters, and the evidence behind each conclusion.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:18.585
This is PRism, an evidence-backed review workspace that connects intent, impact, coverage, and line-level proof in one place.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.501
Connect GitHub, select a repository and pull request, then start an analysis.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:30.626
PRism checks the change through a series of focused review steps.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.220
As the review runs, each check builds toward a useful answer: what changed, where it could break, and which claims still need verification.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.053
The findings register separates verified issues from unverified claims.

00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:56.908
Each finding includes severity and supporting evidence, so reviewers can follow the reasoning.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.174
Open the diff to inspect the exact lines behind a finding.

00:01:04.174 --> 00:01:09.487
Coverage and test evidence show what was exercised, and where confidence is still limited.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.995
Dependency checks can flag assumptions the repository cannot prove, keeping those items visible instead of presenting them as confirmed.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.952
PRism also shows the review method: how facts are collected, how tests run, and how every citation is traced back to the change.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.640
The result is a review someone else can verify, follow, and act on, with the proof kept alongside the conclusion.
